3.19.m:
When the whitewashing is finished, the work of painting is prescribed: / the plinth should be well painted, and the back should be made white with plaster.
It should be executed in accordance with the rules of painting, granting the fulfilment of all desires; / and other marvels should be diligently depicted there.
As the emblem in the temple of Viṣṇu, one should make Garuḍa; / in the temple of Durgā, a lion; and a trident in the dwelling of Hara;
a lotus should be the emblem in the temple of Lakṣmī, a swan in the temple of Brahmā, / a makara for Kāmadeva, and a palm tree for Saṃkarṣaṇa;
a lion standing upon a crescent moon should be in the temple of Āditya; / for the moon, it should be a deer standing inside a circular disc;
one should make a vajra for Śakra, and a staff for Vaivasvata, / likewise a noose for Varuṇa, and indeed a club for Dhanada;
a spear should be made for Skanda, and for Gaṇādhyakṣa, O king, / an axe should be made as the emblem, [while] flame is prescribed for Vahni;

3.20.m:
3.98
Mārkaṇḍeya said:
Now, the characteristics of the temple [precinct]:
In front, in an utterly clean area of ground, one should prepare three mounds / using clay appropriate to the caste along with sand.
Then one should smear the three mounds with fresh cow dung.
In the middle among them is the preliminary consecration mound;
to its south is the worship mound;
to its north is the fire-offering mound.
There, on the preliminary consecration mound, four archways are constructed.
All the mounds are square, four cubits in height, and wide as a third of an archway.
One should surround the two mounds with fresh flags and protective cords.
Having brought wood for the archways from any of the auspicious trees according to the rite prescribed for entering the forest, one should set them up for the mounds.
One must not make an archway from two pieces of wood,
nor should it be stunted;
and all archways must be of equal measure.
At the very beginning, at the western part of the preliminary consecration mound, facing east, the ritual director should not be the owner.
To the south of the ritual director, facing east indeed, should be the Sāttvata;
to his south as well, facing east indeed, should be the knower of the Ṛgveda.
To the left of the ritual director, facing east indeed, is the sacrificer;
to his left, facing east indeed, is the arghabāhu; stationed at the mound, facing south, is the knower of the Yajurveda.
At the mound, in the east, facing west, is the knower of the Sāmaveda;
to his south is the flute player;
to his south as well is the reciter;
to his north is the singer;
behind are the players of stringed instruments.
To the south of the mound, facing north, is the knower of the Atharvaveda.
At the fire-offering mound, facing east, to the west of the fire-shed, is the invoker.
[The shed] is square,
measuring one cubit on all sides, and the fire-shed has stepped tiers; to its north are the guardian of mantras and the guardian of materials. At the worship mound is the Sāttvata who performs the worship;
to his south, facing north, are the guardian of mantras and the guardian of materials.
The astrologer, in another place, possessed of unique knowledge, should be attentive to catching the [auspicious] moment.
For the architect, there is no restriction.

3.21.m:
Thus ends the hundred and first chapter, entitled 'Description of the Preliminary Consecration', in the third book of the venerable Viṣṇudharmottara, in the dialogue between Mārkaṇḍeya and Vajra.
3.102
Mārkaṇḍeya said:
I invoke the living soul, the seed, the all-pervading Lord, / established in five states in the body, and established again in a fivefold manner;
established again in a fivefold way through the nature of wind, / and likewise established in a fivefold way through the nature of fire;
the Lord established in a fivefold way through the nature of water, / and established again in a fivefold way as the essence of the elements;
the Supreme Self, extremely fierce and gentle, the Lord of the world, / all-pervading, supporter of all, Lord of all, unconquered;
infinitely subtle, the boon-giver, the knower of the field, the sovereign Lord, / the Lord of the past, present, and future, bowed to by the worlds;
the substrate of qualities, the womb of the world, greater than the great. / O divine living soul, come forth; enter this image yourself;

may all the earth-supporting mountains arrive for me, lords of immobile things. / I will invoke the trees possessing various forms;
may all the trees arrive, having the swiftness of the mind and the wind. / I will likewise invoke the nether worlds accessible to all;
may all the nether worlds arrive, blameless abodes of the Daityas. / I will invoke the continents, all sustaining marvels;
may the continents arrive, which serve as dwelling places for worlds and are dear to the world. / I will invoke the worlds, delightful dwelling places;
may all the worlds arrive for me, creators of living beings. / I will likewise invoke the oceans dear to the world;
may the oceans arrive for me, repositories of water and dear to the world. / I will invoke here the regions of the earth, shelters of all beings;
and may the regions of the earth, delightful to all, also come here. / I will likewise invoke the four seas;
may the seas arrive swiftly for me, all holding water. / And I will invoke the rivers, boon-granting mothers of the world;

may the boon-granting rivers arrive, auspicious and removing all sins. / I will likewise invoke all the hosts of clouds;
may all the highly fortunate, swift-moving clouds arrive for me. / I will likewise invoke the all-pervading Piśācas; / may all the Piśācas arrive for me, taking form at will.
I will likewise invoke the companions of Skanda;
may the multiform companions of Skanda arrive for me. / I will likewise invoke the attendants of the gods;
may the multiform attendants of the gods arrive for me. / I will likewise invoke the attendants of Hara; / may the multiform attendants of Hara arrive for me.
I will likewise invoke the Mothers, the mothers of the world;
may the divine Mothers arrive, boon-granting, moving everywhere, auspicious. / I will invoke the planets, lords of all the worlds;
may all the radiant planets arrive, lords of the universe. / I will likewise invoke the boon-granting constellations;
